      "id": 1,
      "text": "Shinsaibashi bridge was built in 1622 by merchant Shinsai Okada over the Nagahori‑gawa canal.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shinsaibashi"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1873 a 37.1‑metre, 5.2‑metre arched truss iron bridge, imported from Germany, replaced the original wooden span.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shinsaibashi"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The 1909 stone bridge became Osaka’s first, featuring a double arch and four‑leaf clover railings carved into its balustrade.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        18,
        19
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shinsaibashi"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "At night the stone bridge glowed beneath eight gas lamps, later repurposed for a pedestrian overpass after the canal was reclaimed.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        20,
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shinsaibashi"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Shinsaibashi‑suji is a covered shopping street north of Dōtonbori, running parallel to Mido‑suji and stretching eastward.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shinsaibashi"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "West of Mido‑suji, Amerika‑mura offers an American‑themed shopping scene that has become Osaka’s youth‑culture hub.",
